between Ukraine and Russia and Europe so that not only we end this war, we create a new framework and a new understanding” that would secure “decades, if not centuries, of peace”. The head of Moscow’s delegation, Vladimir Medinsky, an aide to Russian President Vladimir Putin, said he hoped Moscow’s “bigger and more professional” team would “enable us to move forwards on the path towards peace”. A source in the Russian team said the Ukraine settlement was “merely one item on the agenda”, with the main objective “the creation of a comprehensive and indivisible security architecture for the entire continent”. The delegation includes, for the first time, diplomat Sergei Ryabkov, who issued Moscow’s ultimatum to the West on European security before the 2022 invasion. Ukraine’s top negotiator Rustem Umerov said Kyiv’s priority remained securing a ceasefire and discussing security guarantees. “Our people are waiting to end this war,” he said. Before the talks, Ukraine’s delegation met the US envoys and European and North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) officials, including NATO’s top commander in Europe, Gen Alexus Grynkewich, who is also taking part. In a joint statement, they reaffirmed their “shared commitment to a strong post-war European security architecture”. Europe has been alarmed by Trump’s plans, and the European Union (EU) said on the eve of the talks that “meaningful” security discussions could only take place after a ceasefire. Putin has refused broader ceasefires and face-to-face talks with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky. Trump hinted ahead of the talks that Ukraine might have to give up land, saying: “They’re going to end up with a little bit different shape, Ukraine.” Russia occupies large parts of Ukraine but has not seized the whole of the Donetsk region, and demands that Kyiv surrender the areas it still holds there. Russia pounded Ukraine in the run-up to the talks, with Kyiv saying 55 people were killed in a frontline village as they queued for humanitarian aid. The Ukrainian military said Russia fired 162 drones and one ballistic missile overnight into Tuesday. “We don’t have any particular expectations,” a senior Ukrainian official told AFP. — AFP In case the Kuwait Times carries an article from an Arabic or any other daily, proper attribution must be given both to the Kuwait Times

